# Conventions — {{project_name}}

> One hop from `AGENTS.md`, which wins on any conflict. Elaboration and
> worked examples only — the binding rules live there.

## Debugging

- Every entry point (CLI, server, test target, frontend page) gets a
  `.vscode/launch.json` profile (or your IDE's equivalent) when it is
  created, so the next debugger doesn't reverse-engineer it.
- Canonical hosts, ports, and env vars live in README §Debug. Check
  there before attaching; add what's missing — don't guess or hardcode.
- When parts run together (front end + back end), README §Debug says
  how to start both (one command, or the order), how the front end
  reaches the back end in development (dev-server proxy, CORS setting,
  or an API-URL env var), and which launch profile attaches to both at
  once (VS Code `compounds`, or your IDE's equivalent).
- Frontend local-file access during debug: temporarily expand
  `vite.config.*` `server.fs.allow` (or the equivalent) and drive
  uploads from in-page JS — native file dialogs break headless and
  DevTools-driven debugging. Revoke the expansion when the session ends.

## Communication examples

- "Per D-073 we capped X" → "Per D-073 (the retry-budget cap) we capped
  X" — the reader doesn't have the decision log memorized.
- "We switched to RPC" → "We switched the inter-process call layer to
  RPC (HTTP+JSON over a local socket)"; the short form is fine after.
- "Performance is better" → "p50 dropped from 240ms to 90ms on the smoke
  task" — show the difference, don't adjective it.
- When pasting code, inline-comment any variable whose meaning isn't
  self-evident inside the snippet; the reader lacks your file context.
- If you'd have to look a phrase up in six months, rewrite it now.

## Alias mechanics

Alias files in the repo root (`CLAUDE.md`, `GEMINI.md`, legacy
`AGENT.md`) are one-line pointer files containing exactly `@AGENTS.md`.
Claude Code and Gemini CLI read an `@path` line as "include that
file's full text here", and a regular file works on every OS and git
setting. A symlink with the relative target `AGENTS.md` also works
where it survives, but git checks a committed symlink out as a plain
text file on Windows clones without `core.symlinks`. A file holding
only the bare word `AGENTS.md` is such a checkout: it loads nothing, so
replace its content with `@AGENTS.md`. Tools that read `AGENTS.md`
directly (Codex CLI, Cursor) need no alias; tools with neither
auto-discovery nor imports are bridged by a symlink or by their own
config pointed at `AGENTS.md`. Duplicating content across alias files
is the failure mode this pattern exists to prevent.

## Paths and state

- Repo-internal references stay relative so the docs survive a clone to
  any machine; `${PROJECT_ROOT}` indirection also works.
- Genuinely environment-bound references (another local repo, a system
  service, a tool install location) carry a `(local-env)` tag so readers
  know the line will not travel.
- `.agent_works/` holds only the docs `AGENTS.md` §2 routes there, and
  they travel with the repo. Anything else kept there shows up in
  every search beside live code, describes the project as it was, and
  carries no rank that tells a later session to distrust it.
- When working files go: a review written for the user to choose from
  stays in `.local/` until they choose, then the unfixed findings
  become handoff lines and the file goes; a benchmark script and its
  data go once the answer is given; screenshots, logs, and run
  transcripts go once the result is reported. A script that generates
  an image the app ships is product code (`scripts/`), not a working
  file.
- `.local/` stays on this machine. If unfinished work that reads a
  working file will continue on another clone, say so and ask whether
  to commit the file under `docs/`.
- Local model weights go in the root `models/` folder (gitignored as
  `/models/`, so a code folder such as `src/models/` is unaffected) with
  a download script and setup notes in README.

## README vs AGENTS.md

`README.md` is the human's entry point — optimize it for clone-and-run
in ~5 minutes: what the project is, install, configure, run, verify, and
the debug addresses / ports. `AGENTS.md` is the agent's entry point —
routing, rules, working principles. Same project, two audiences, no
content overlap. For README craft, browse the curated examples at
<https://github.com/matiassingers/awesome-readme>.
